
Log Analysis with Regular Expressions


📌 Project Overview

This project contains an automated data-processing pipeline written in Python that parses system logs (syslog.log) from an internal ticketing service named ticky.
The script uses advanced regular expressions to track operational statistics, identifies error trends, calculates service utilization metrics per user, and exports the data into clean CSV sheets. Finally, a companion script converts these CSV sheets into live, responsive HTML data tables for monitoring dashboards.

🛠️ Features & Tasks Completed

• Regex Log Parsing: Evaluates structural signatures for INFO and ERROR alerts while capturing explicit messages and target usernames.
• Data Aggregation: Collects total counts for unique error types and builds split user metric mappings (INFO vs ERROR counts).
• Sorted Data Pipeline:
	• Organises systemic errors from most frequent to least frequent.
	• Organises user statistics alphabetically by account names.
• Reporting & Visualization: Generates standard CSV reports and translates them cleanly into interactive HTML tables.

📂 File Architecture

text
├── syslog.log          # Raw system log containing service events
├── ticky_check.py      # Core Python parser & metrics aggregator (Created)
├── csv_to_html.py      # CSV engine to HTML generator utility
├── error_message.csv   # Target spreadsheet: System error frequencies
├── user_statistics.csv # Target spreadsheet: Usage data per team member
└── /var/www/html/      # Web server directory hosting final dashboards
    ├── error_message.html
    └── user_statistics.html
Use code with caution.

🚀 Execution & Setup Guide


1. Make the scripts executable

bash
chmod +x ticky_check.py csv_to_html.py
Use code with caution.

2. Run the processing automation pipeline

bash
sudo ./ticky_check.py
Use code with caution.

3. Generate the web dashboard metrics

bash
sudo ./csv_to_html.py error_message.csv /var/www/html/error_message.html
sudo ./csv_to_html.py user_statistics.csv /var/www/html/user_statistics.html
Use code with caution.

